{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774104539,"nonce":"8ei5eV4TcnjyMDD9baP48a/HV9Y=","title":"Auto-start races past max_concurrent when state.json not yet written","message":"## Bug\n\nWhen two or more labeled issues exist in the tracker, the watcher can launch more sessions than `max_concurrent` allows.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`count_active_sessions()` counts sessions by reading `state.json` files from the sessions directory. But `_launch_background()` is non-blocking — it spawns `launch.py` as a subprocess. There is a window between the subprocess spawn and `state.json` being written where the new session is invisible to `count_active_sessions()`.\n\nTimeline:\n1. `check_new_issues()` polls tracker, finds issues A and B (both labeled)\n2. Within the same call: launches A, `active_count += 1` → correctly skips B\n3. Next loop iteration (30s later): `count_active_sessions()` reads session dirs\n4. If A's `launch.py` hasn't written `state.json` yet → `active_count` = 0\n5. `_known_issue_ids` prevents re-launching A, but B is new → launches B\n6. Now both A and B are running despite `max_concurrent: 1`\n\nThe `_recently_launched` dict already tracks recently launched SIDs, but `count_active_sessions()` doesn't factor it in.\n\n## Fix\n\nInclude `_recently_launched` entries in the active count. If a SID was launched within a grace window (e.g. 60s) and has no `state.json` yet, count it as active. This closes the TOCTOU gap without requiring synchronous launch.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `count_active_sessions()` (or the caller) accounts for recently launched sessions without `state.json`\n- [ ] Test: two labeled issues, `max_concurrent=1` — only one launches per iteration even if `state.json` is delayed\n- [ ] Existing tests still pass","files":null}]}